# Hand-written type seed fed to Spinel via `--rbs` (see spin build and the CI
# golden check). Unlike test/git-stack.rbs.expected -- which is the *emitted*
# golden and must never live under rbs/, or it would seed itself -- this file is
# an input: signatures we assert so the analyzer keeps these methods on the
# concrete fast path.
#
# The single shared `effective_parent_rule` (bin/git-stack.rb) threads one
# parent-resolution rule through both the subprocess wrappers and the
# StackContext traversal. That unifies these methods' branch-name parameters
# with the rule's result and, left to inference alone, widens them to Spinel's
# untyped slow path. Each takes a branch (or parent) name -- always a String --
# so pinning them here is a faithful assertion, not a fib to the compiler; it
# keeps the compiled binary unboxed and the emitted golden free of new untyped.
#
# `effective_parent` is pinned for the same reason one step out: it takes the
# trunk LIST (it answers "is this branch itself a trunk?" before applying the
# rule), and an `Array[String]` parameter inferred from scratch widens to
# `Array[untyped]`, which then flows into the rule and back out through
# `effective_parent_of`.
#
# `containing_trunk` and `restack_subtree` are the trunk-list pair: the first
# picks the trunk a branch rests on, so its untyped result would spread through
# every reparent it feeds (`set_parent`, `record_reparent_base`, `reparent!`);
# the second now takes the whole list rather than one caller-chosen trunk, and
# widens the same way `effective_parent` does. Both are faithful: a branch name
# and the trunk list, String and Array[String] at every call site.
#
# The `StackContext` builders and the top-level `children_of` are the newest
# members of the same family: a context is now built FROM the trunk list (it
# drops a trunk's recorded parent as it parses the config), so the list crosses
# `build` / `build_topology` / `load` and, uninferred, widens the same way --
# taking `@trunk`, `effective_parent_rule`, and `effective_parent_of` with it.
#
# Everything below the first four entries was added when Spinel moved to
# 0b8527df4b87, whose inference propagates that same unification further than
# the previous pin did: without them the emitted golden carries widened
# `class Object` signatures (`paint`, `green`, `set_trunks`, `is_trunk?`,
# `clear_parent`, `clear_base`, `untrack!`) that were concrete before. They are
# the same kind of assertion as the original four -- branch names, colour text,
# and the trunk list, all String / Array[String] at every call site -- and with
# them the golden is back to zero untyped in `class Object`.
#
# `stack_root` / `would_cycle?` / `validate_new_parent!` moved from here to
# `class StackContext` below when they became methods on the context. They still
# need pinning -- the trunk LIST parameter widens to `Array[untyped]` exactly as
# `effective_parent`'s does -- so the move relocates three assertions rather than
# retiring them; they simply lost the leading `StackContext` parameter.
class Object
  def sh: (String) -> String
  def checkout!: (String) -> nil
  def branch_exists?: (String) -> bool
  def ahead_behind: (String, String) -> Array[Integer]
  def paint: (String, String) -> String
  def green: (String) -> String
  def set_trunks: (Array[String]) -> Array[String]
  def is_trunk?: (String, Array[String]) -> bool
  def containing_trunk: (String, Array[String]) -> String
  def effective_parent: (String, Array[String]) -> String
  def clear_parent: (String) -> bool
  def clear_base: (String) -> bool
  def untrack!: (String) -> nil
  def restack_subtree: (String, Array[String], bool, String, StackContext) -> nil
  def children_of: (String, Array[String]) -> Array[String]
end

# `parent_of` / `children_of` join `branch?` for the same reason. Note that the
# seed reaches methods but NOT instance variables: `@parents` and `@children`
# still emit as `Hash[String, untyped]` / `Hash[untyped, untyped]` under this
# Spinel (they were `Hash[String, String]` before). That widening is exactly
# what `StackContext#children_of` and `#walk_order` predicted in their comments,
# and it is why their `.to_s` guards before `.split` are now load-bearing rather
# than defensive -- removing them costs six more untyped signatures.
class StackContext
  def branch?: (String) -> bool
  def parent_of: (String) -> String
  def children_of: (String) -> Array[String]
  def stack_root: (String, Array[String]) -> String
  def would_cycle?: (String, String, Array[String]) -> bool
  def validate_new_parent!: (String, String, Array[String], String) -> nil
  def load: (String, Array[String]) -> nil
  def self.build: (Array[String]) -> StackContext
  def self.build_topology: (Array[String]) -> StackContext
end
